08/11 00:56:09 - OpenCompass - INFO - ChatCompletion(id='chatcmpl-a7f9a6f0c1c4777b', choices=[Choice(finish_reason='stop', index=0, logprobs=None, message=ChatCompletionMessage(content="**Summary: DHS FY2019 Appropriations Report**\n\nThis Congressional Research Service (CRS) report analyzes the legislative process and final funding levels for the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) for Fiscal Year 2019 (FY2019). The report compares the enacted FY2018 appropriations, the Trump Administration's FY2019 budget request, committee recommendations from the House and Senate, and the final enacted legislation contained in the Consolidated Appropriations Act, 2019 (P.L. 116-6).\n\nThe appropriations process for FY2019 was marked by significant delays. Following the Administration's February 2018 request for $47.43 billion in adjusted net discretionary budget authority, neither the House nor the Senate passed standalone DHS appropriations bills. Instead, Congress relied on continuing resolutions (CRs) that maintained FY2018 funding levels. When the final CR expired on December 21, 2018, DHS experienced a partial government shutdown lasting 35 days. Funding was restored in January 2019 via a short-term CR before the final consolidated bill was signed into law on March 23, 2019.\n\nThe final enacted legislation (P.L. 116-6) provided DHS with **$49.41 billion** in adjusted net discretionary budget authority. This represented a **3.5% increase ($1.69 billion)** over FY2018 enacted levels and a **$2.04 billion increase** over the Administration's original request. Notably, the final bill included **$12 billion** for the Disaster Relief Fund (DRF), significantly exceeding the Administration's $6.65 billion request, and appropriated $165 million in Overseas Contingency Operations (OCO) funding directly to the Coast Guard.\n\nThe report details funding across five legislative titles:\n*   **Title I (Management & Operations):** Received $1.88 billion, a 37.7% increase over FY2018, driven largely by a $51 million provision for financial systems modernization.\n*   **Title II (Security, Enforcement, & Investigations):** The largest component, receiving $40.00 billion. This title covers Customs and Border Protection (CBP), ICE, TSA, the Coast Guard, and the Secret Service. Funding increased by 4.1% over the request and 1.2% over FY2018.\n*   **Title III (Protection, Preparedness, Response, & Recovery):** Received $18.27 billion, a 42.3% increase over the request. This substantial rise was primarily due to the increased DRF allocation for FEMA.\n*   **Title IV (R&D, Training, & Services):** Received $1.62 billion, a modest 3.3% increase over the request, covering USCIS, FLETC, and the Science and Technology Directorate.\n*   **Title V (General Provisions):** Included $303 million in rescissions of prior-year funds and provisions directing $300 million of unobligated DRF balances to offset new appropriations.\n\nThe report also highlights the Department's transition to a Common Appropriations Structure (CAS) to improve transparency and standardize budgeting across components. Additionally, it provides technical context on budgetary concepts such as discretionary versus mandatory spending, offsetting collections, and the application of the Budget Control Act (BCA) caps, specifically noting the use of the disaster relief cap adjustment mechanism to accommodate the increased FEMA funding.", refusal=None, role='assistant', annotations=None, audio=None, function_call=None, tool_calls=[], reasoning=None), stop_reason=None, token_ids=None)], created=1786380927, model='Qwen3.5-27B', object='chat.completion', service_tier=None, system_fingerprint=None, usage=CompletionUsage(completion_tokens=758, prompt_tokens=8426, total_tokens=9184, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), prompt_logprobs=None, prompt_token_ids=None, kv_transfer_params=None)

