08/11 00:58:35 - OpenCompass - INFO - ChatCompletion(id='chatcmpl-b287f42f7bdfafb5', choices=[Choice(finish_reason='stop', index=0, logprobs=None, message=ChatCompletionMessage(content='**Summary of Congressional Compensation Report**\n\nThis report details the constitutional framework, historical evolution, and legislative mechanisms governing the compensation of Members of the U.S. Congress. Grounded in Article I, Section 6, and the Twenty-Seventh Amendment, the latter of which mandates that any salary changes cannot take effect until after an intervening election, the document outlines how congressional pay is ascertained and funded. Since Fiscal Year 1983, salaries have been drawn from a permanent appropriations account. As of the most recent adjustment in January 2009, the standard salary for Senators, Representatives, Delegates, and the Resident Commissioner of Puerto Rico is $174,000. Leadership positions command higher rates: the Speaker of the House earns $223,500, while the President pro tempore of the Senate and the majority and minority leaders in both chambers earn $193,400.\n\nThe report identifies three primary methods for adjusting Member pay. Prior to 1968, adjustments required specific legislation. From 1967 to 1989, a quadrennial commission recommended increases for top federal officials, a process utilized three times before being replaced by the Ethics Reform Act of 1989. Currently, the primary mechanism is an annual automatic adjustment based on the Employment Cost Index (ECI), which measures private-sector wage changes. Under this system, adjustments take effect automatically unless Congress statutorily prohibits them, revises them, or if the General Schedule (GS) federal employee pay increase is lower, in which case Member pay is capped at the GS rate.\n\nA significant portion of the report analyzes the frequent statutory prohibitions of these automatic adjustments. Since the implementation of the ECI formula in 1990, Congress has denied or frozen pay increases in the majority of years. Specifically, adjustments were accepted 13 times but denied 16 times between 1994 and 2019. These prohibitions are typically embedded in annual appropriations bills or continuing resolutions rather than standalone legislation. For instance, potential increases ranging from 0.9% to 2.6% were blocked annually from 2010 through 2019, often citing the need to match or freeze GS employee pay. Consequently, if Members had received every ECI-prescribed adjustment since 1992, the 2019 salary would have been approximately $210,900; instead, it remained at $174,000.\n\nThe report also highlights the political context surrounding these freezes, noting that inflation-adjusted salaries have decreased by 15% between 2009 and 2019. Furthermore, it catalogs numerous legislative proposals from the 112th through the 116th Congresses aimed at altering the compensation system. These proposals include repealing automatic adjustments, linking pay to legislative productivity (such as passing a budget resolution), reducing salaries, or withholding pay during government shutdowns. While some measures, such as holding salaries in escrow if a budget is not passed by a certain date, were enacted, they were often rendered moot by subsequent legislative actions. The report concludes by referencing related CRS products regarding retirement benefits, health insurance, and historical salary data, emphasizing the complex interplay between constitutional constraints, economic formulas, and political will in determining congressional compensation.', refusal=None, role='assistant', annotations=None, audio=None, function_call=None, tool_calls=[], reasoning=None), stop_reason=None, token_ids=None)], created=1786381072, model='Qwen3.5-27B', object='chat.completion', service_tier=None, system_fingerprint=None, usage=CompletionUsage(completion_tokens=703, prompt_tokens=8601, total_tokens=9304, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), prompt_l